--- !u!114 &2589322523726563233
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "<b>Subsurface Scattering</b> simulates light that is able to penetrate material and escape in multiple directions. \r\n\r\nYou can use it to make organic and translucent materials, like skin and vegetation. HDRP implements subsurface scattering using a screen-space blur technique.\r\n\n1. In the <i>Project</i> Window, select the <b>Bamboo_Leaf_A</b> material.\n\n2. In its <i>Inspector,</i> in the <b>Surface Options</b> section, switch the <b>Material Type</b> from <b>Subsurface Scattering</b> to <b>Standard.</b>\n\n3. Notice that the light cannot penetrate anymore through the leaves, as the material is now fully opaque.\n\n4. Undo your change.\n\n5. In the <b>Surface Inputs</b> section, experiment with the <b>Thickness Remapping</b> slider to alter the transmission of the light defined by the <b>Thickness map.</b>"
--- !u!114 &5458079510542063532
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: 'Materials are used to set the visual aspect of the objects in the Scene. Typically, a Material relies on a shader and a variety of imputs, such as textures and numerical values.


      HDRP uses <b>Physically Based Rendering (PBR)</b> materials. PBR is an industry-standard technique that more accurately simulates the real-world interaction of the lighting with the materials.'
